From 204bf713b16a9aa8c4bc7f1061abefde67735785 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 23:05:30 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08080/11362] [outer-loop] GPU layer-split sweep: splitting is
 free and dominates the CPU-offload lever

Ran the queued 5-arm sweep. Both 3090s idle, conductor stopped, port clear;
all four preconditions checked and recorded. All three pre-registered gates pass.

                     tok/s   peak/card MiB   aggregate MiB   imbalance
  single ctx32768    38.51          20428           20428          -
  cpu12  ctx32768    15.47          18282           18282          -
  split2 ctx32768    38.90          10788           21412     164 MiB
  single ctx81920    38.60          22900           22900          -
  split2 ctx81920    38.84          12384           24604     164 MiB

Layer-splitting is FREE: +1.0% decode at 32768 and +0.6% at the shipped 81920,
while cutting peak per-card residency 47.2% / 45.9% (-9640 / -10516 MiB), with
1.3-1.5% imbalance. `-ts 1,1` was passed explicitly because llama.cpp's default
splits by AVAILABLE VRAM; the evenness gate is what proves the ratio took effect.

The CPU-offload lever in use today costs 59.8% of decode to save 10.5% of
per-card VRAM. Layer split strictly dominates it on both axes.

THE CAVEAT THAT TRAVELS WITH THE HEADLINE: aggregate VRAM across both cards goes
UP when split (+984 / +1704 MiB), because each card carries its own compute
buffers and non-layer state. The win is PER-CARD headroom, not total memory. On
a single-GPU host there is no win -- there is a small loss.

Why it matters operationally: the shipped n_ctx 81920 DOES fit one 3090 (22900
of 24576) but with only 1676 MiB spare, tight enough that the MTP draft head
plus fragmentation is a real risk. Split leaves 12192 MiB free per card.

Two secondary findings:

- The CPU-offload arm's PREFILL fell 908 -> 195 tok/s (-78.5%), worse than its
  decode fall. The 2026-07-28 characterisation of that lever as costing "58-86%
  of decode throughput" understates its impact on prompt-heavy work, which ARC
  induction is.
- The 164 MiB imbalance is IDENTICAL in both split arms despite their per-card
  shares differing by ~1.6 GB -- a fixed asymmetry, not a proportional one, so
  it should not grow with context or model size. Observation, not verified
  against the tensor map.

CONTROL ARM: arm 1 was written to reproduce 2026-07-28's 36.07 tok/s @ 21416
MiB, measured on Q4_K_M; this ran on the newly-shipped QAT. VRAM landed at 20428
(-988), matching the 993 MiB file-size difference and the head-to-head's
independent 20430 MiB to within 2 MiB. Decode landed at 38.51 tok/s, +6.8% --
INSIDE the +-10% window, so the throughput target effectively DID reproduce. The
correction in b44fb77cf, which retracted the claim that both halves would fail
to reproduce, is confirmed by the run.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT ESTABLISH: nothing was measured on Kaggle's 4xL4, which is
what the scored submission runs on; induction QUALITY under split was not
measured; and one run per arm means the ~1% throughput differences are within
plausible noise -- read split as not-slower, NOT as genuinely faster.

